  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe cooler temperatures will continue today, very similar to\nyesterday's temperature, with high surface pressure moving in. \nThanks to this high pressure rain is not expected today. This \nwill all change as we head into tomorrow. A cut off upper low \nover the western CONUS will meet a trough digging down from \nCanada over us on Thursday. This will provide synoptic support \nfor our next best chance for rain. The primary surface features \nwith this look to be associated the the upper level forcing \nassociated with the western CONUS. This keeps the main low well \nto our south. Therefore, we look to be on the northern edge of \nthe precipitation shield. There is good agreement in this setup \nacross the global models and now that it is getting closer CAMs.\nWhere the variability remains though is how far north the edge \nof this gets. There is good agreement in southern Minnesota \nseeing some greater than 0.5 inch amounts as both the LREF and \nREFS have 50 percent or more of membership showing this. It is \nfarther north up to the Twin Cities and into Eau Claire that \nthere is a difference. The REFS takes this 50 percent of a half \ninch line up to the Twin Cities and Eau Claire while the LREF \ndoes not. RRFS and HRRR have a similar split. So the heavier \nrain getting up farther north is a possibility, but with mainly \nthe RRFS/REFS family of models as the main support it seems like\na upper quartile like solution rather than the most likely one.\nLooking ahead this is our main chance for any precipitation \nuntil mid to late next week.\n\nHigh pressure will move into the Northern PLain on Friday \nbehind Thursday's rain. This will put us into a region of pretty\nstrong CAA. This will provide a setup for a windy day on Friday\nand a very cold overnight period Friday into Saturday. Looking \ninto guidance a widespread frost looks very likely. In addition \nmuch of guidance has a freeze possible to likely across the \nregion with the exception of the urban core of the Twin Cities \nmetro. After a cold start Saturday will warm up quickly as a \nridge is building into the central CONUS. The WAA with this will\ncontinue throughout the weekend bringing Saturday highs up into\nthe lower 60s and Sunday's into the mid 60s. A developing \ntrough over the western CONUS next week, while the ridge remains\nover us will provide for more WAA opportunities Monday to\nWednesday. This will allow for highs to break back into the \nlower 70s next week. So overall a bit of a roller coaster ahead \nwith the coldest air of the season late this week followed by a \nreturn to temperatures about 10 degrees above normal the next \nweek. This is typical for the transition seasons in the Upper \nMidwest where we get alternating periods of warmer and cooler. \nEventually the cold will win out, but the warm doesn't go \nwithout a fight.",
